From fa1b3989e568f7dc910c57f0baa28490c07a8712 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 14:34:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] test(mac-shell): every script can start Arbor as a person
 does

A script's Arbor is the bare binary, a child of the script's shell: it has the Accessibility permission from its first instant and borrows it from
whoever started the shell. A person opens Arbor.app, which answers for itself. macOS treats them differently: on hosted Macs an app that was given the
permission while it waited was told that a key which was down was up, at 233 looks of 233; one opened with the permission already there the truth, 314 of
314, as the shell's child, 288 of 288. So a held key was a tap in an Arbor that people have, and in no script's.

138 of the 159 scripts start the bare binary (scripts/mac-shell/how-started.py counts them), all but two of them through two doors: arbor_launch in lib.sh
and arbor_start_apps in lib-apps.sh. With `run.sh --as-a-person late|early` (ARBOR_AS_A_PERSON), on GitHub's Macs, both doors open Arbor.app through Launch
Services with the environment a child would have had.

  late    arbor_start forgets the entry, must see Arbor waiting, and gives it the permission while it waits: a first day, and every update of a build without
          a certificate. arbor_relaunch finds the entry in place, as on the day after.
  early   the entry is there before every start: any other day.

Off unless asked for; a rehearsal is not changed by it. Every start now says in the output how Arbor was started, so that a result carries its own witness.
lib-person.sh, by which two scripts say it for a start of their own, keeps its two names and becomes two lines: there is one way of doing it.

run.sh: the guard against a branch whose lib.sh is older than a switch looked with `git show | grep -q` under pipefail, which fails when grep leaves early and
more of lib.sh lies behind the match than a pipe holds: on the trunk's lib.sh (67 KB behind it) once in 80, with this change's 5 KB more 39 times of 40 for --release
and 38 of 40 for this switch. It reads to the end now.
